Sipeed -- Tang Console 138K schematic
-------------------------------------
Source: Tang_Mega_138K_Console_32001C__Schematics.pdf ("Tang_138K_Console_V32001C", Rev 1.3;
        https://dl.sipeed.com/shareURL/TANG/Console/02_Schematic).  The
        document states no licence or terms of use.
Used:   board/console138k/tango_console138k_sdram.cst: the 38 SDRAM ball locations are the SDRAM0_* nets of sheets
        SOM_BTB1 (p5), FPGA_EXT_CONN (p8) and SOM_BTB0 (p4: SDRAM0_~{CS} = F21), DQML / DQMH = DM0 / DM1; LVCMOS33
        because VCCIO2 is the 3.3 V rail (sheet POWER_TREE, p2).  The file is the Tango authors' (MIT).
        board/console138k/tango_console138k.cst and tango_console138k_dbg.cst, the BL616 link block: the ball
        locations (JTAG.TMS T13, JTAG.TCK V12, JTAG.TDI R13, JTAG.TDO U13 = BL616 GPIO_0..3; FPGA_UART.TX U15 ->
        BL616_IO27_RX; FPGA_UART.RX V14 <- BL616_IO28_TX; sheets SOM_BTB0, SOM_BTB2 and USB_JTAG), LVCMOS33 / BANK_VCCIO
        3.3 because VCCIO10 and VCCIO5 are on the 3.3 V rail (sheet POWER_TREE), and the board's resistors that the pull
        settings take into account (R72 / R73 on JTAG.TDO, sheet USB_JTAG).  The pulls, drives, the jtagseln constraints
        (NET_LOC V_JTAGSELN, Gowin SUG1018-1.6E 2.10.2; IO_LOC T16) and the spi_sck_clk global clock are the Tango
        authors' settings, each with its reason in the file.  Both files are the Tango authors' (MIT).


A2600Nano (MiSTle-Dev) -- earlier reference only
------------------------------------------------
https://github.com/MiSTle-Dev/A2600Nano (GPL-3.0), commit fbc50ec46b75e86e79df7e8846025a616446abd5.  The BL616 link
block of tango_console138k.cst / _dbg.cst was first taken from its src/tang/console138k/a2600_top.cst (lines 1 and
6-21), the first core to run the BL616 link and JTAG hand-back on this board.  The block is now the Tango authors' own
text (MIT, above under Sipeed): its values are unchanged, because they are the ones proven on silicon, and their
sources are Sipeed's schematic and Gowin's documentation, cited in the file.  Its comments and layout are new; the
statements are Gowin's constraint syntax, and four port names (jtagseln, bl616_jtagsel, spi_csn, spi_irqn) are the
same as A2600Nano's.
Also:   board/console138k/tango_sys_top138.v: the JTAG hand-back (a top-level port jtagseln = NOT bl616_jtagsel, NET_LOC
        V_JTAGSELN) was first seen in A2600Nano (a2600_top.vhd line 321); the file cites Gowin's JTAGSEL_N documentation
        (UG290-2.7.6E Tables 4-2 and 7-3, SUG1018-1.6E 2.10.2) for it.  tango_link.v: the SD STATUS reply follows the
        byte layout of A2600Nano's src/misc/sd_card.v.  Both files are the Tango authors' code (MIT).


