Gowin Semiconductor -- taken from the user's Gowin EDA installation, not in this repository
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Nothing of Gowin's is in this repository.  A build takes these from the Gowin EDA IDE it runs on (GOWIN_HOME, default
~/gowin: board/console138k/build_core.sh, ddr3/test/build_ddr3_test.sh, sim/run_tb_retune.sh; README "Building"),
under the licence of that installation:
  - PLL_INIT: IDE/ipcore/PLL_ADV/data/PLL/pll_init.v (module PLL_INIT; the framework is built and tested with the
    V1.9.11.03 file, sha256 3ef05a1ba92157d73e3c54ed9d589645508a4ae332d8bcf133a0457cc3e866c8).  Every build adds it:
    every GW5AST PLL needs it to lock, and the framework's PLL wrappers (and some cores' own PLLs) instantiate it.
    sim/run_tb_retune.sh compiles it from the same place.
  - The Gowin DDR3 Memory Interface IP (TANGO_VIDEO_DDR3 builds): ddr3/tango_ddr3_ip.tcl has the IDE generate it
    into each build.  Its configuration follows Sipeed's DDR3 example for the board.
  - The device primitives (PLL, PLLA, OSER10, DCE, ...) are the toolchain's; their names, parameters and ports are
    Gowin's, documented in Gowin's user guides (the PLLs: UG306, Arora V Clock User Guide).
The PLL modules board/console138k/pll_c27.v, pll_c27_mod.v, pll_c48.v, pll_c48_mod.v, pll_c1485.v, pll_c1485_mod.v,
pll_ddr3.v, pll_ddr3_mod.v and gowin_pll_sdram.v are
the Tango authors' (MIT): each instantiates the PLL primitive with its settings, and each parameter is
commented from UG306.  They replace earlier files that were Gowin IP generator output, with the same module names,
ports, instance names and parameter values (the post-place-and-route netlists and bitstreams of old and new are
identical).
